WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.800
Everyone has different tastes in movies, but
now and then a film comes along that just

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.500
defies understanding no matter which way you
slice it.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:12.340
Whether it's a high-concept art film or just
the product of a ridiculous plot, these movies

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.640
stick around for one reason: they leave us
more confused than ever when the credits finally

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:17.140
roll.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.640
Spoilers coming up!

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.000
Inland Empire

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.570
David Lynch's 2006 film Inland Empire is an
insane sort of beast.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:31.160
A three-hour nightmare of a movie featuring
Hollywood, street prostitutes, and a super

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.900
weird sitcom about rabbits, it makes Mulholland
Drive look like an episode of Friends.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.360
It's also as close as Lynch has ever come
to making a full-on horror movie, providing

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.860
scares more startling and perplexing than
he's ever dared before.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:46.780
But what does it actually mean?

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.280
According to The Guardian, it's "about whatever
you come away thinking it's about."

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.960
So... looks like you got this one right!

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.400
The Prestige

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.100
Christopher Nolan's movie about dueling magicians
is pure cinematic sleight-of-hand, a mystery

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.380
that keeps all its clues in plain sight.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:07.580
Still, there's one aspect of the movie—the
ending—that continues to leave viewers confused.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:13.220
The ending shows the length Angier will go
to for his magic when Christian Bale's Borden

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:19.600
discovers a macabre storeroom of Angier's
own dead, duplicated bodies, still in the

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.460
boxes where they drowned during each performance.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.480
But who is the real consciousness, and the
real Angier?

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:29.960
It's a brain-twister, but Tesla revealed the
answer before the final act of the movie,

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.750
when they discover the field of cloned hats.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.060
"Don't forget your hat."

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.340
"Well, which one is mine?"

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.420
"They are all your hats, Mr. Angier."

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:46.020
All of the Angiers are the "real" Angier,
exact copies who share the exact same memories,

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.460
motivations, and a fear of death.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:54.299
Over and over again, he dies for applause,
always leaving behind a living copy of himself

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.020
who's doomed to drown screaming in a box the
following show.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.700
Having a twin would have been so much easier.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.240
Under the Skin

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.540
This alien abduction movie is unlike any other
in the sci-fi genre.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:11.300
It's truly only an abduction movie for the
first half—after which the alien, played

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:16.040
by Scarlett Johansson, becomes disillusioned
with luring humans into traps.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:21.800
When she decides to let one victim go free,
the alien goes rogue, curious about this planet,

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.770
its people, and her role as an alien in disguise.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:29.530
The second half of the movie follows the alien
trying to grapple with a growing sense of

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.500
her own humanity, and the vulnerability that
comes with it.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.320
Her journey is cut short when a lumberjack
tries to rape her in the woods.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:41.840
But when Johansson's human skin starts coming
off, the attacker burns her alive.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:47.780
Look past the sci-fi aspects, and this is
essentially the story of a young woman's self-discovery—and

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.480
her devastating, fatal realization that the
human world is harsh, unfair, and full of

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:52.980
horrors.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:54.540
Gerry

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.370
Gus Van Sant's Gerry is basically a movie
about nothing.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:01.530
It's about two guys named Gerry who get lost
hiking in the desert.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:05.920
They wander around the endless landscape,
blaming each other for their screwups, until

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.680
one Gerry kills the other Gerry and gets out
of the desert.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.320
It's dry stuff, and in the end, the movie
doesn't do anything.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.920
But that's really the point of it: the two
protagonists could be anyone, and their journey

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:17.820
could be to anywhere.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:23.660
In this way, Gerry is sort of a universal
story, serving as a mirror for viewers, reflecting

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:28.750
the petty conflicts and the damage we do to
each other and ourselves—and finally, yes,

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.380
the overwhelming boredom of life.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:31.940
Filth

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.920
Starring James McAvoy as corrupt cop Bruce
Robertson, Filth starts off as a comedy and

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.860
murder mystery, then steadily veers off course
into a film about psychological torment.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.680
The tonal shift is so dramatic that it's no
surprise viewers can end up losing track of

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.340
the plot, particularly during the last-act
reveal that Bruce has been spending his nights

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.480
roaming the streets dressed as his own estranged
wife.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:00.540
While on one of his cross-dressed jaunts,
he witnessed the murder he's supposed to solve—a

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.120
fact he can't reveal without incriminating
himself.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.060
In the final moments of the film, even though
Robertson has a shot at redemption, he chooses

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:14.080
not to fix anything and hangs himself—the
ultimate kiss-off from a selfish, self-preserving

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:14.580
man.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.000
Eraserhead

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:21.940
David Lynch's bizarre first feature film is
a little easier to comprehend than its reputation

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:22.440
suggests.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.400
At its core, it's a story about the terror
of being a new parent.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:31.880
From the opening scene to that monster baby,
to Henry's visions of the baby taking his

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.370
place, it all goes back to the nightmare of
fatherhood.

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:37.020
Or at least, that's how most people look at
it.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.840
Lynch himself has said the movie is open to
interpretation, saying that "no one, to my

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:44.100
knowledge, has ever seen the film the way
I see it."

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.780
Which is exactly the point.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.460
Sometimes with movies, it's not about plot
logic.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.300
It's all about how it makes you feel!

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:51.920
"Can I borrow a feeling?"

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.600
"HAHAHA.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.800
"Can I borrow a feeling…

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:57.520
HAHAHA."

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:58.040
Thanks for watching!

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.960
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.970
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

